1
00:00:00,160 --> 00:00:01,360
Welcome to Catholic Futurist.

2
00:00:01,600 --> 00:00:10,960
Today we are examining a significant essay that was released last month by Dario Amade, the CEO of Anthrapic, titled The Adolescence of Technology.

3
00:00:11,840 --> 00:00:43,200
Joining me to discuss the philosophical and spiritual implications are Brian Green, who serves as Director of Technology Ethics at the Marcula Center at Santa Clara University, and Brett Robinson, who serves as Associate Director for Outreach and Associate Professor of the Practice at the McGrath Institute for Church Life at Notre Dame, and Devin Patel, a legal and political strategist in Washington, DC, and adjunct law professor at Notre Dame and is the visiting fellow for tech ethics and democracy at the Joseph Raney Center for Public Policy in DC.

4
00:00:44,320 --> 00:00:45,680
So thanks guys for joining.

5
00:00:54,800 --> 00:01:05,680
So we're having this conversation in the shadow of the recent news that one of Anthropic's top AI safety researchers has quit with a cryptic warning that the world is in peril.

6
00:01:06,400 --> 00:01:10,960
He cited concerns about AI, bio weapons, and the state of the wider world.

7
00:01:12,400 --> 00:01:23,760
So given that this essay is enormous and we've had a lot of questions that could go in all sorts of directions, myself and the team, we've come up with a few sections that we really want to focus on with all of you.

8
00:01:23,920 --> 00:01:31,520
And so here I'm going to read an opening segment from Dario's essay here, and it's referencing Carl Sagan's book and the film version of it.

9
00:01:32,000 --> 00:01:40,320
And he quotes, the international panel interviewing her asks, if you could ask the aliens just one question, what would it be?

10
00:01:41,360 --> 00:01:44,720
Now her reply is, I'd ask them, how did you do it?

11
00:01:45,280 --> 00:01:46,000
How did you evolve?

12
00:01:46,640 --> 00:01:51,360
How did you survive this technological out adolescence without destroying yourself.

13
00:01:52,080 --> 00:02:04,320
Now, when I think about where humanity is now with AI, about what we're on the cusp, I believe we are entering a rite of passage, both turbulent and inevitable, which will test who we are as a species.

14
00:02:04,960 --> 00:02:14,880
Humanity is about to be handed on almost unimaginable power, and it is deeply unclear whether our social, political and technological systems possess the maturity to wield it.

15
00:02:16,560 --> 00:02:20,400
That's quite a, that's quite an enormous chunk, and there's a lot there.

16
00:02:20,720 --> 00:02:24,400
But I want to ask, what were your initial impressions of this piece?

17
00:02:26,640 --> 00:02:33,680
Amade seems to have supreme confidence in the technocratic paradigm that using our minds we can go through this rite of passage and succeed.

18
00:02:34,480 --> 00:02:37,360
Do you think that confidence is warranted given humanity's history?

19
00:02:38,240 --> 00:02:40,240
Brian, if I may, could I start with you?

20
00:02:41,440 --> 00:02:41,600
Sure.

21
00:02:42,640 --> 00:02:48,960
I think one thing I would say would be to back up and think about once again this framing of the essay.

22
00:02:49,200 --> 00:02:50,960
It's not the adolescence of humanity, right?

23
00:02:51,120 --> 00:02:52,640
It's the adolescence of technology.

24
00:02:53,520 --> 00:02:59,040
And so this framing of the entire thing is very technocratic, as you say.

25
00:03:00,160 --> 00:03:05,360
We might ask ourselves, is this really all the solution that we need?

26
00:03:06,080 --> 00:03:18,640
And considering the larger framework that surrounds this entire essay, I think it then becomes more apparent why the particular things that he brings up are brought up.

27
00:03:18,800 --> 00:03:32,000
But I guess what I wanted to get to, I guess with that first question and kind of the framing is that Dario speaks that we're in this moment of technological adolescence and that we're in this rite of passage and that there's so many things that can happen.

28
00:03:32,640 --> 00:03:36,160
And he seems uniquely confident in the technocratic idea.

29
00:03:36,880 --> 00:03:46,640
The idea that with technology we can solve a lot of these issues, but of course from a Catholic framework, and of course this is Catholic Futurism, you know, we're interested in that Catholic perspective and and there might be points of disagreement with that.

30
00:03:46,880 --> 00:03:51,040
And so just asking, you know, maybe for you to comment on kind of that, that wider theme.

31
00:03:51,760 --> 00:03:51,840
Yeah.

32
00:03:53,120 --> 00:04:03,040
So in addition to what I said previously, what I would say is that he does come at this from a very secular technocratic perspective.

33
00:04:03,280 --> 00:04:05,840
We can't really expect him to come at it from a different perspective.

34
00:04:07,440 --> 00:04:11,840
So the culture of Silicon Valley is very much a bubble in many ways.

35
00:04:13,520 --> 00:04:15,600
It has many cultures, subcultures within it.

36
00:04:16,080 --> 00:04:21,360
However, it's very cut off and isolated from the rest of the world, I would say.

37
00:04:22,720 --> 00:04:25,040
And a lot of people in Silicon Valley already recognize that.

38
00:04:25,200 --> 00:04:30,960
They recognize that there's a cultural divide between Silicon Valley, particularly the AI companies, and the rest of the world.

39
00:04:32,800 --> 00:04:36,960
This has led some people to think that, hey, we have everything we know.

40
00:04:37,360 --> 00:04:38,640
The rest of the world just is ignorant.

41
00:04:38,720 --> 00:04:39,760
We don't need to listen to them.

42
00:04:40,800 --> 00:04:46,160
On the other hand, some people within these tech companies look at the rest of the world and they say, Oh, we're missing something.

43
00:04:46,320 --> 00:04:47,680
There's something that we need to recognize.

44
00:04:48,800 --> 00:04:51,600
Some other sources of wisdom that perhaps we need to be aware of.

45
00:04:52,560 --> 00:05:00,240
I think that some of the companies in Silicon Valley have recently started recognizing that they do need to talk to outsiders that they are living inside this bubble.

46
00:05:01,200 --> 00:05:13,600
And so folks at Anthropic, for example, reached out to myself and some of my colleagues and we then reached out to other folks in the Catholic Church and we went and visited them just last month to go talk about these sorts of issues.

47
00:05:14,240 --> 00:05:19,200
And if you read Claude's Constitution, you can see our names down there as outside readers on the Constitution.

48
00:05:20,160 --> 00:05:24,960
So while they are recognizing that they're in a bubble and they're that they're approaching things in a very particular way.

49
00:05:26,160 --> 00:05:32,480
I think there's also an opportunity here for outsiders to get in there and tell them what they're missing.

50
00:05:33,200 --> 00:05:34,640
Are they thinking about the deeper things in life?

51
00:05:34,800 --> 00:05:35,680
Are they thinking about the meaning?

52
00:05:35,840 --> 00:05:40,480
Are they thinking about the context of the world they are living in and the world that they're creating?

53
00:05:41,200 --> 00:05:51,040
And there are a lot of resources that the Catholic Church brings to bear on these issues that we just have the resources.

54
00:05:51,200 --> 00:05:52,240
I'll just put it that way, right?

55
00:05:53,280 --> 00:06:05,440
When we, when they say, hey, we found this tension between between utilitarism or the kind of traditional effective altruism perspective on things and how the model actually wants to operate.

56
00:06:06,400 --> 00:06:08,000
And they move towards virtue ethics.

57
00:06:08,640 --> 00:06:14,400
That is an acknowledgement, I think, that they recognize that their philosophical stance, their previous philosophical stance was inadequate.

58
00:06:14,640 --> 00:06:19,440
And they've moved towards a stance which is more in tune with the Catholic Church, for example.

59
00:06:20,240 --> 00:06:26,000
When you talk about virtue ethics, the Catholic Church has got, you know, thousands of years of work that's been done on that.

60
00:06:26,960 --> 00:06:29,920
And there are just a lot of other ways that we can help them.

61
00:06:30,400 --> 00:06:42,880
And so when they are reaching out to us, we are really in a good position to actually share some of that knowledge with them and help guide them towards what they, you know, what they could be if they were realizing their potential in a more full way.

62
00:06:46,240 --> 00:06:46,880
Thank you, Brian.

63
00:06:47,040 --> 00:06:51,520
And then, Brett, what were your immediate responses upon reading Dario's essay?

64
00:06:51,840 --> 00:06:55,200
What stood out to you when you, when you looked over the contents?

65
00:06:56,720 --> 00:07:01,440
Yeah, I mean, if I could just react to the quote you provided, which I think is part of that framing.

66
00:07:02,800 --> 00:07:10,320
This idea that there's a desire to hear from the higher intelligence what we're supposed to do, right?

67
00:07:10,480 --> 00:07:26,640
The aliens in this case from Sagan's story, which I think betrays the real desire that they have to, as Brian said, sort of listen outside their own bubble and seeking something, I don't know, almost transcendent.

68
00:07:26,880 --> 00:07:40,800
So it's kind of strange to me the way it's framed that in a number of places Amede kind of rejects kind of religious interpretation of these things, warns against the language of doom or salvation.

69
00:07:42,080 --> 00:07:51,600
And I think he kind of throws the baby out with the bath water, because there's clearly a desire there to understand this in a larger frame and not just an imminent kind of technocratic frame.

70
00:07:52,560 --> 00:08:07,120
So this invocation of the aliens is kind of interesting and funny, but again kind of self contradictory because later he says, you know, we shouldn't be relying on sci-fi narratives and these kinds of things to guide our development and all of this.

71
00:08:07,440 --> 00:08:26,240
So he seems kind of trapped in a, I don't know, kind of a closed loop that he's created in this argument, which I think is symptomatic of the kind of closed loop or feedback loop that he even references in AI itself, which is kind of learning from itself and developing along these lines.

72
00:08:26,480 --> 00:08:46,720
So I found these contradictions interesting because not because, I mean, they were sort of not great argument, but also because I think they reveal something about the latent desire that these developers have to understand something outside of this frame, but he doesn't quite get there.

73
00:08:50,480 --> 00:08:52,560
And Devin, what stood out to you?

74
00:08:52,880 --> 00:08:53,360
Any thoughts?

75
00:08:54,320 --> 00:08:59,680
Yeah, reacting both to the opening passage you mentioned as well as Brett and Brian's comments.

76
00:09:00,880 --> 00:09:03,680
I consider myself a bit of a hopeful realist.

77
00:09:05,120 --> 00:09:12,640
And so that, and living and working in Washington in politics and policy that really strains on the hopefulness at times.

78
00:09:13,520 --> 00:09:30,640
So reading Amade's essay here, a lot really struck me as unfortunately realistic as far as, and I'm sure we'll get to the sort of geopolitical implications of this essay and his suggestions and comments.

79
00:09:31,600 --> 00:09:36,000
But we'll put those ideas aside for the moment.

80
00:09:37,920 --> 00:09:41,200
It was sort of similar to Brett's comments.

81
00:09:41,680 --> 00:09:47,360
I was struck a little by what I call a bit of a gnosticism, right?

82
00:09:47,920 --> 00:09:51,760
And this is this is found and picked up in different parts of the essay.

83
00:09:52,000 --> 00:09:58,000
One that's that's standing out to me right now because of Brian's comments is the sort of AI constitution section.

84
00:09:59,360 --> 00:10:12,960
And I believe there was a comment that he made along the lines of sort of this constitution being kin to a sealed letter or sealed or an unopened letter from a deceased parent that contained a letter.

85
00:10:12,960 --> 00:10:22,080
that contains this sort of information, these secrets to solving or getting out of or maturing through this age, this adolescence.

86
00:10:23,120 --> 00:10:32,400
And I've had conversations, as I'm sure everyone here has had, with lots of folks in Silicon Valley, with engineers and C suite leadership.

87
00:10:33,040 --> 00:11:02,400
And it's interesting how a number of lead engineers have spoken to me about what they view as their productive philosophy, pro-human philosophy, and with one even going so far as to say that they consider themselves in one cohort in one of these labs, Christians without Christ in how they approach their philosophy in developing these technologies and avoiding sort of the harms and the bats.

88
00:11:03,040 --> 00:11:16,800
And so I do think that there is a a sort of there is hopefulness in that there is a group of folks out there who are looking to something beyond themselves.

89
00:11:17,120 --> 00:11:19,280
I think they're a little lost and they can use direction.

90
00:11:19,600 --> 00:11:33,200
And I think that as Brian Brett said, the Catholic Church offers so many resources that can really square these circles, at least not of which, right, putting the Christ back in Christianity seems like an easy one, but I'll leave it at that for now.

91
00:11:35,680 --> 00:11:36,320
Thank you, Devin.

92
00:11:36,720 --> 00:11:49,120
And Brian, I'd like to come to a question, it's directed at you, but then everyone please respond as well, but the idea, like one of the things that stood out very clearly is that the sacred is not mentioned in this essay and there is no mention of God.

93
00:11:49,920 --> 00:12:00,960
And when speaking to, you know, AI ethicists and people working in the major AI labs, how do they define good when you're having a conversation around, you know, what making Claude good or making him good?

94
00:12:00,960 --> 00:12:02,160
good or making God harmless.

95
00:12:02,640 --> 00:12:14,000
Of course, those are ideas and values that, you know, from a Catholic perspective, from a Christian perspective, we're oriented towards a metaphysical understanding of reality and then the nature and orient itself as well towards meaning.

96
00:12:14,320 --> 00:12:22,160
But when God is not mentioned in this essay, you know, I found that to be quite an elephant in the room in that sense.

97
00:12:22,320 --> 00:12:28,720
And Brian, if you could speak maybe towards that and how those conversations go and also what you make of that more broadly.

98
00:12:29,360 --> 00:12:29,440
Yeah.

99
00:12:29,680 --> 00:12:35,680
I think, first of all, no one in the tech industry is going to say anything about God publicly.

100
00:12:35,920 --> 00:12:49,360
I mean, it would be nice if they would be willing to do that, but for the most part, even if they would be interested in doing that, they don't feel like they can because business is supposed to be completely secular.

101
00:12:51,520 --> 00:12:59,120
So we can have these aspirations and hopes, but very often, you know, religion is just bracketed and it's shoved to the side and we're going to pretend it doesn't exist.

102
00:12:59,520 --> 00:13:12,000
Now, if you're talking about the question of good, what I would say is that they basically have a secularised Christian ethic a lot of the time, not exclusively, sometimes it's much more pagan ethic where it's just like, if you're powerful, you do what you can.

103
00:13:13,360 --> 00:13:18,000
And so I would say that within Silicon Valley there is a tension between these kind of two sides.

104
00:13:18,160 --> 00:13:27,840
There's a kind of a secularized Christianity, a secularized Christian ethic, which sees it recognizes human dignity and recognizes maybe human rights.

105
00:13:28,800 --> 00:13:31,360
It might construe these things in a utilitarian fashion.

106
00:13:32,560 --> 00:13:55,280
But I think that also, as I noted with the Constitution, the Constitution is very much their way they're looking at it now is a document for moral formation and virtue, which is completely different than the utilitarian perspective you would expect coming from Anthropic specifically because a lot of the people who founded the Anthropic did come from an effective altruistic background.

107
00:13:57,680 --> 00:14:09,200
The other thing to consider would be that this more pagan side of things, this the strong do what they will, and the weak suffer what they must, is an ethic in Silicon Valley.

108
00:14:09,280 --> 00:14:14,320
And it's found in most of the world also still in places that have not been exposed to Christianity.

109
00:14:15,040 --> 00:14:21,600
So when we look at China, for example, on their development of technology, we should expect that's exactly the approach that they're going to take.

110
00:14:22,000 --> 00:14:24,240
The strong would do what they can, and the weak suffer what they must.

111
00:14:25,040 --> 00:14:31,920
And this is something that we need to psychologically adjust ourselves to as Christians because we're not generally used to it a lot of the time.

112
00:14:32,560 --> 00:14:51,360
And so in the process of struggling with other places in the world, geopolitically speaking, we also need to be aware of the fact that this ethic that we're opposed to is one that is going to potentially contaminate our own judgment and perspective on a lot of issues.

113
00:14:52,320 --> 00:14:56,000
So I guess if I would sum it up, I would say, how can they understand good?

114
00:14:56,560 --> 00:15:09,040
There are the for those in the tech industry who have the more secularityized Christian perspective, they are recognizing that there are some deeper fonts of wisdom that they can be tapping into.

115
00:15:09,680 --> 00:15:15,120
And so once again, like I said, they've come to various, you know, Christian groups, the Catholic Church.

116
00:15:15,760 --> 00:15:19,280
They're looking to other religions now as well because they want to be luralistic.

117
00:15:20,400 --> 00:15:28,720
But I think that from the first exposure to some of the ideas that we brought to them when we talked to them, the overlap is strong.

118
00:15:29,040 --> 00:15:45,760
And I think the recognition that Western culture basically has a secularized Christianity there as a strong undercurrent makes it clear to those in the tech company that, oh, this maybe we can get a deeper understanding of what we're trying to do if we just go back to the source of where it all came from.

119
00:15:49,360 --> 00:15:50,080
Thank you, Brian.

120
00:15:51,440 --> 00:16:05,040
And another question that we have here is looking at the essay, and one of the themes that seems to be quite prevalent throughout the essay is this assumption that intellect is on some level kind of the ultimate definition of what it means to be human.

121
00:16:05,680 --> 00:16:16,320
And that does feel, you know, quite, I'm, you know, nervous to use this term, but quite Luciferian in that nature of this, you know, this desire for intellect and that being kind of the ultimate value.

122
00:16:17,040 --> 00:16:21,920
How, when you guys look at that, how does that counter what the church defines as the human person?

123
00:16:24,000 --> 00:16:26,560
Yeah, it seems rather excarnate.

124
00:16:27,600 --> 00:16:35,600
I think at one point he addresses the lack of embodiment that AI has and that we share as human beings.

125
00:16:37,040 --> 00:17:08,080
But I think the first move, which was to abstract intelligence from a much more complex matrix of embodiment of environment of context of subjectivity of will is kind of a foundational error and a building of an idol that that they've assigned these kind of formal signs to, like, genius in your pocket or a country of geniuses.

126
00:17:10,160 --> 00:17:19,120
And I think this is really problematic semiotically because it structures the way that we then talk about these things and the way we interact with them.

127
00:17:20,960 --> 00:17:30,560
And so I think that that sort of metaphorical slippage is dangerous because, as we know, it's not it doesn't have true intelligence.

128
00:17:31,600 --> 00:17:46,480
We use that term as kind of a placeholder, but understanding how the the sort of semiotic environment of AI, the interface structures our interaction with it, and as Brian said, there's no reference to the effect on humans, right?

129
00:17:46,720 --> 00:17:48,800
It's all done within the frame of technology.

130
00:17:49,120 --> 00:17:56,000
So the adolescence of technology without asking sort of where we are and what the effects are on us.

131
00:17:57,200 --> 00:18:14,720
And so that enclosed sort of semiotic loop that's created by suggesting that these things are in all sorts of places in the document, he anthropomorphizes AI as, you know, a good person or something with a psychology or a country of geniuses, et cetera.

132
00:18:16,000 --> 00:18:28,400
And I feel like those and I'll leave this to the philosopher, but are that there's a lot of category errors that are being committed that I think destabilize this conversation and get us into some weird cul de sacs.

133
00:18:31,840 --> 00:18:31,920
Yeah.

134
00:18:32,880 --> 00:18:35,040
Brian, I don't know if you if you'd like to respond.

135
00:18:35,680 --> 00:18:36,240
Yeah, I agree.

136
00:18:36,240 --> 00:18:44,960
I mean, the whole artificial intelligence discussion has been framed in a way which is very problematic from the beginning.

137
00:18:45,280 --> 00:18:48,400
And these category errors permeate the entire conversation.

138
00:18:48,960 --> 00:19:06,480
It's very difficult to connect a lot of these contemporary ideas about the way intelligence and agency and other ideas like that are existing in the tech industry as opposed to existing in the tradition coming from the Catholic tradition, for example.

139
00:19:08,240 --> 00:19:14,560
And so when we're faced with these category errors, we have to first of all ackknowledge them, recognize that there's a problem.

140
00:19:15,200 --> 00:19:21,760
And then when we recognize the problem, we have to take it to the next step and say, what do we do about this problem?

141
00:19:22,000 --> 00:19:22,960
How do we actually solve it?

142
00:19:23,280 --> 00:19:31,120
Going back to a richer understanding of what these terms mean is the first step, or the second step if the first step is recognizing it.

143
00:19:31,680 --> 00:19:38,400
And then after that, we have to figure out what do we actually need to do in order to solve the problem.

144
00:19:40,240 --> 00:19:46,960
in terms of changing our thinking and recognizing that the world is not set up in the way that they're describing it.

145
00:19:48,320 --> 00:19:50,240
And ultimately, it's kind of a conversion, right?

146
00:19:50,480 --> 00:19:59,360
You need to actually get into issues of how do we shift thinking in the tech industry towards a more richer understanding of humanity and the universe.

147
00:20:01,600 --> 00:20:02,320
No, thank you.

148
00:20:02,640 --> 00:20:11,520
So here I'd like to move on to kind of arc number two and to kind of piggyback on what you were mentioning, Brett, with that country of geniuses, I'm going to quote here Dario.

149
00:20:12,080 --> 00:20:19,680
So he says, suppose a literal country of geniuses were to materialize somewhere in the world in roughly 2027.

150
00:20:20,000 --> 00:20:25,920
Now imagine, say, 50 million people, all of whom are much more capable than any Nobel Prize winner.

151
00:20:26,400 --> 00:20:32,560
In addition to just being a smart thing you talk to, it has all the interfaces available to a human working virtually.

152
00:20:33,120 --> 00:20:38,800
It does all of these tasks with, again, a skill exceeding that of the most capable humans in the world.

153
00:20:40,160 --> 00:20:40,240
Wow.

154
00:20:41,520 --> 00:20:42,480
That's quite extraordinary.

155
00:20:43,360 --> 00:20:45,360
So here, there's a question that we received.

156
00:20:45,600 --> 00:20:48,800
So Amade imagines this country bringing global abundance.

157
00:20:49,200 --> 00:20:54,320
However, Catholic teaching stresses that abundance must be rightly ordered.

158
00:20:54,800 --> 00:21:07,440
If this country is built on the brittle logic of secularism and material reductionism, can its gifts ever actually satisfy human hunger or will they only increase our spiritual poverty by making us dependent on a machine savior?

159
00:21:09,600 --> 00:21:12,720
Devin, if I if I may potentially address that question to you.

160
00:21:13,600 --> 00:21:13,760
Sure.

161
00:21:14,640 --> 00:21:30,240
Well, when I was reading this section, I was reminded of Soviet Russia and the sort of cultural attempts of Soviet Russia to become dominant in in many different fields during the, the height of the Cold War.

162
00:21:31,040 --> 00:21:50,240
And, you know, without a doubt, the Soviets produced beautiful music accomplished symphonies and and ballet acts and had advances and great advances in science and you know you could go on and on.

163
00:21:52,480 --> 00:22:12,560
But the way that history treats this era or regards these sort of these developments, these these sort of cultural gifts that this supposedly, you know, this behemoth of a nation provided is.

164
00:22:12,720 --> 00:22:37,440
is, you know, it is still rooted in that moral corruption, the moral bankruptcy of Soviet communism that was clearly antitheistic that, you know, not only rejected any sort of Christian antropology, but actively, uh, weaponized the state against those with that antropology, right?

165
00:22:38,320 --> 00:22:45,040
We don't, we also don't need to recall the fact that the Soviet Union was the first nation in the world to legalize abortion.

166
00:22:46,240 --> 00:22:47,600
All these things go hand in hand.

167
00:22:48,000 --> 00:23:13,360
where the richness of a state, whether it's a country of geniuses or actual people, I think is seriously hindered by where it lacks in this moral development, in this moral rooting, and without that, without the tradition of the Jewish and Christian heritage, I don't think you can go very far.

168
00:23:14,320 --> 00:23:28,160
Given now the kind of AI country of fifty million geniuses versus the Soviet Union, I think eff all in all, it would still end up in the same place.

169
00:23:33,440 --> 00:23:49,760
Yeah, there's a If you guys have read Brian Christian's book The Alignment Problem, there's a story about Amade, and he's like 15 years ago, when he's programming models to try to win all sorts of video games and generalize across video games.

170
00:23:49,840 --> 00:23:52,880
And he's doing games that involve races.

171
00:23:53,040 --> 00:24:05,680
And in one of these, there's a boat race and his boat keeps crashing into the dock and exploding because in the game, you're rewarded for going to the dock and getting power ups and so on.

172
00:24:05,920 --> 00:24:10,640
So the model got confused and thought, if I just get these infinite power ups, then that's a good thing.

173
00:24:10,960 --> 00:24:23,120
And that seems like a metaphor that you lose sight of the race or the telos or the end of what we mean by abundance when we're just thinking about acquiring reward or whatever.

174
00:24:23,440 --> 00:24:36,720
And we get into a situation where we take something like embodied work, which depends on what this questioner's definition of abundance is, I guess, but is there abundant work?

175
00:24:37,840 --> 00:24:45,440
to produce abundant fruit for human beings who participate in that work as a common work, I think, which would be the Catholic view.

176
00:24:47,440 --> 00:25:02,400
If we're talking about something like Amadeus talking about in the economic sense, like disembodied consumption, where we just get a universal basic income because the machines are doing all the work, to me, that's not abundance, that's poverty.

177
00:25:04,560 --> 00:25:13,920
But because he's using almost strictly political and economic definitions for what success and safety and so on looks like in this scenario.

178
00:25:15,760 --> 00:25:31,840
Again, we're talking past each other in a sense, but I think to Brian's point, like naming the fact that some of these categories are an error and developing a shared grammar and terminology is sort of the first step in trying to bridge some of these gaps.

179
00:25:33,760 --> 00:25:41,920
But I think that's where there's still a bit of a chasm between what the church would think of abundance and what perhaps an AI engineer thinks of abundance.

180
00:25:45,360 --> 00:25:49,760
If I could say just a few things relating to the Catholic social tradition.

181
00:25:50,160 --> 00:26:04,160
I mean, this is something that Catholic social teaching going all the way back to Leo XIII has been talking about, which is that the Industrial Revolution brought forth material from factories ennobled and at the same time degraded the human beings who were involved in the process.

182
00:26:06,240 --> 00:26:13,600
There's something strange about material abundance and spiritual impoverishment which comes along with it.

183
00:26:15,280 --> 00:26:22,000
It, for some reason, this has been a long ranging trend in history now for well over a century.

184
00:26:22,640 --> 00:26:24,400
And we might ask ourselves, why is this?

185
00:26:24,400 --> 00:26:28,880
Why is it that when we are materially richer, we become spiritually poorer?

186
00:26:30,720 --> 00:26:34,000
What Bret was just saying about universal basic income is absolutely correct.

187
00:26:34,160 --> 00:26:45,520
I think it's one of the worst possible solutions to helping people in the future if the economy is transformed, because it ultimately says, you have no purpose in this world.

188
00:26:45,840 --> 00:26:48,640
We're just giving you stuff because we don't want you to die, basically.

189
00:26:50,160 --> 00:26:53,920
And think about, could you send a worse message to your population?

190
00:26:54,960 --> 00:26:57,600
And of course, from the Christian perspective, it's absolutely false.

191
00:26:58,560 --> 00:27:00,800
It's every human being has infinite dignity.

192
00:27:01,120 --> 00:27:09,680
We are made to the image and likeness of God, and therefore we need to recognize that in each other, or else we are going to create a terrible, terrible future.

193
00:27:11,600 --> 00:27:25,760
When Devin brought up the Soviet Union, I mean, this is once again something that we need to be aware of, that things can go dramatically wrong in the world if we don't think about things correctly, and small mistakes can lead to very bigger mistakes later on.

194
00:27:29,200 --> 00:27:36,800
Connecting it all back to the essay, what I would say is that there are some mistakes in the essay in its conceptualization of humanity and reality.

195
00:27:38,720 --> 00:27:46,080
The question is, do these mistakes get the chance to balloon into the disastrous future that would be possible?

196
00:27:46,960 --> 00:27:48,560
And there are lots of bad options, right?

197
00:27:48,720 --> 00:27:59,200
There are all the bad options that he lists in the essay, and then there are additional bad options, which he calls unknown unknowns, but I think that we as Christians coming at this from the outside could say, you know what?

198
00:27:59,440 --> 00:28:04,240
There are actually some big spiritual problems going on here too that we need to recognize.

199
00:28:05,200 --> 00:28:11,200
And so if we step back and recognize what those issues are and we bring them to the forefront and we say, hey, we found these problems.

200
00:28:11,360 --> 00:28:29,200
We really think you need to be thinking about them that actually gives us a better chance of creating a better future together as opposed to an almost certain chance of things not going well if we don't come forward and tell the truth, the ultimate truth that we believe about reality based on our worldview that we're coming from.

201
00:28:30,240 --> 00:28:34,240
Things are not going to work out well if we don't participate in this conversation to the fullest extent.

202
00:28:37,600 --> 00:28:37,840
Thank you.

203
00:28:38,640 --> 00:28:48,240
One of the things that's very clear from the essay that I picked up and I think maybe is quite unique in historical context is to have a rising GDP with rising unemployment.

204
00:28:48,880 --> 00:29:08,160
And I think that to that point of UBI and that being obviously a very suboptimal solution, it seems, given, I mean, the World Economic Forum was just a few weeks ago and you had many of the leaders from the leading AI labs there speaking and they were speaking directly with some of the political leaders from around the world.

205
00:29:08,400 --> 00:29:23,920
And I think it seems quite, quite clear that there isn't, you know, a, this is going to be an incredibly intense couple of years in a very short time frame, a amount of transformation that, of course, that the principal vector is that technological change.

206
00:29:24,880 --> 00:29:38,080
And the political, you know, it seems that there is no solution without some type of political current that understands that people are, you know, that the, that what people need is not just, you know, an economic package here, but I think that requires a real formation.

207
00:29:38,320 --> 00:29:48,000
And it seems quite clear that a lot of the people developing these AI systems and developing and leading them, don't have a formation where that whole human is, is front and center.

208
00:29:48,080 --> 00:29:58,960
When you, when you remove intelligence from, from the embodiment, when you, when you talk about efficiency as something that is, you know, to be privileged almost exclusively, it seems quite, quite stressful.

209
00:29:59,920 --> 00:30:06,000
And to that idea of reductionism in that sense, here's a quote from Dario.

210
00:30:06,880 --> 00:30:18,800
Powerful AI could contribute to enormous advances in biology, neuroscience, economic development, global peace, and it's common for regulations to backfire or worsen the problem.

211
00:30:19,920 --> 00:30:25,920
And given that, it's easy to say, no action is too extreme when the fate of humanity is at stake.

212
00:30:27,440 --> 00:30:36,880
Now, my big question with regards to that is, of course, to maybe try to take a wider view and also to understand the enormous pressure and incentives that these leading AI labs are under.

213
00:30:37,280 --> 00:30:49,600
I mean, there's a big picture, there's an enormous geopolitical race going on between the United States and China, and those are very different political, economic and social and moral perspectives coming from those.

214
00:30:49,920 --> 00:30:55,920
And then I understand, of course, that the leading AI labs are under enormous pressure to arrive to AGI first.

215
00:30:57,120 --> 00:31:11,520
Given that and understanding that if the fate of humanity is at stake or if taking the whole pie is at stake, the incentives will make it that people will drive there fast without thinking about the wider perspective or thinking about, ultimately, the individual at the bottom.

216
00:31:12,320 --> 00:31:12,400
What?

217
00:31:14,000 --> 00:31:16,560
What are we as Catholics supposed to do and to think?

218
00:31:17,360 --> 00:31:25,920
What are we supposed to, how are we supposed to respond to that when you have many of these leaders speaking like this and we understand the enormous stakes that there are and the enormous pressure that they're under.

219
00:31:27,040 --> 00:31:39,280
I'll take a stab and say that we've got people of faith, Catholics, Christians, we have to understand that we are running both a sprint and a marathon at the same time.

220
00:31:40,800 --> 00:31:55,040
As Amede points out in his essay, as we've talked about, you know, formation is the real issue here, but we don't have time for that kind of longitudinal formation in our labs, in the culture.

221
00:31:56,240 --> 00:31:59,600
But I think we still need to endeavour to do that.

222
00:32:00,160 --> 00:32:02,640
Number one, the right, run the marathon.

223
00:32:04,240 --> 00:32:13,760
On the other hand, we also need to be running this sprint and do what I'm kind of coining right now is kind of crash course formation.

224
00:32:14,880 --> 00:32:20,880
And that's with our policymakers, that's with folks in the academia and the think tanks.

225
00:32:21,040 --> 00:32:39,280
It's folks in the labs who are amenable to these conversations, right, who have this, um, secularized Christian worldview already that maybe they, they, they've sort of already opened the door for a sort of nitty gritty, uh, uh, education and formation on these issues, right?

226
00:32:39,600 --> 00:32:45,040
The fact that in this essay and in all these conversations around labor that we're not even talking about the dignity of labor, right?

227
00:32:45,280 --> 00:32:51,360
That the, the, the creative nature, uh, of man and labor with God, um, is, is completely absent.

228
00:32:51,440 --> 00:33:05,120
And we're just already skipping to UBI and how we can solve away the economically, um, you know, disadvantageous or, you know, useless people in the AI economy that these labs and developers see.

229
00:33:06,480 --> 00:33:09,280
We're clearly missing a huge piece of that conversation.

230
00:33:10,720 --> 00:33:21,600
And so, you know, it's going to take the work of the church, of parishes, of folks who have the ears of community leaders, of lawmakers.

231
00:33:22,480 --> 00:33:28,000
Every day, folks can call their lawmakers' offices and raise these issues with them.

232
00:33:28,800 --> 00:33:36,800
But without the voices of those who have been formed, we there's no chance.

233
00:33:37,440 --> 00:33:48,560
in pushing the conversation in a way that actually does lead to positive cultural outcomes and a more safeguarded version of AI development.

234
00:33:49,840 --> 00:33:56,160
And if I could follow up on that, I think, I mean, policy and regulations and law only go so far.

235
00:33:56,400 --> 00:34:27,360
I think, Ben, maybe what you're pointing to as well is the fact that, I mean, we're not going to reform the minds and morality of a million AI developers in, you know, six months, but what we can do is focus on our own formation and, you know, a great kind of a source for thinking about this is Ramona Guardini who in his kind of two volumes set The End of the Modern World and then Power and Responsibility says, look, this is about not a political program but a program of personal conduct and formation.

236
00:34:27,680 --> 00:34:41,680
And I think, if I may, advocate for sabotage in the kind of old sense of the French sabot, the wooden shoe that came to symbolize a kind of slow and clumsy work around this increasing mechanization.

237
00:34:42,800 --> 00:34:48,560
I think you're starting to see this even in Christian schools and classical schools.

238
00:34:49,120 --> 00:34:57,440
where the pressure is to adopt the ed tech, adopt the AI, train the children early, get them up to speed on this because this is the way of the world.

239
00:34:58,400 --> 00:35:02,720
And saying, actually, in a sort of act of sabotage, no slow down.

240
00:35:03,600 --> 00:35:07,760
There's an ancient wisdom here that we're actually going to study more intentionally.

241
00:35:08,640 --> 00:35:18,000
And if you read the letter from Shawarma, who left OpenAI, the safety engineer, he's moving to the UK to study poetry and write.

242
00:35:18,480 --> 00:35:19,520
Now, what does that tell you?

243
00:35:20,560 --> 00:35:20,720
Right?

244
00:35:20,960 --> 00:35:22,160
Is he just a romantic?

245
00:35:22,400 --> 00:35:23,520
And, you know, no.

246
00:35:24,000 --> 00:35:30,880
It tells you that at the root of all this is human language and purpose and meaning and how we express ourselves.

247
00:35:31,040 --> 00:35:44,640
And I think this whole sort of semiotic psyop that AI has put on around creating the image, the idolatrous image of genius and country of genius and so on is a distraction.

248
00:35:45,760 --> 00:35:51,040
And we have to reclaim the language and the terminology and integrate the grammar that they're using.

249
00:35:51,440 --> 00:36:08,960
And I think the way to do that is to think and live liturgically, which is to do the work of the people, the common work of the people together, oriented towards the telos of a theological order and not towards a political order, a technological order, a technocratic order.

250
00:36:10,000 --> 00:36:12,960
Those things fall into place if you're pursuing the right ends.

251
00:36:17,360 --> 00:36:20,560
I love the phrase semiotic psyop.

252
00:36:20,800 --> 00:36:23,600
I'm going to try to figure out how to use that as much as I can in the future.

253
00:36:25,360 --> 00:36:28,720
This question about formation is something that the folks in the tech industry are very aware of.

