00:40:36,800 --> 00:40:44,240
They've already the Chinese Communist Party has already started to employ AI in their military.

276
00:40:44,880 --> 00:40:48,480
They just debuted a fully autonomous underwater.

277
00:41:24,800 --> 00:41:34,640
It's not hard to see how willing and able that these autocracies are to leverage these technologies so quickly, so broadly.

278
00:41:38,320 --> 00:41:51,200
Yeah, this reminds me of the irony of the Internet, which was supposed to be this great democratizing medium and only led to the rise of many of these autocracies, seems to have an inverse effect.

279
00:41:51,440 --> 00:42:11,360
And I just wonder if, and I'm not endorsing autocracy, but I'm wondering if AI as a medium is inherently autocratic in its structure and the environment it creates so that it actually benefits state regimes that actually use it in this way.

280
00:42:11,680 --> 00:42:25,600
Whereas in a democracy, you know, we can say we live in a democracy, but we also are living in a bit of an oligarchy when it comes to the control of technology, which is getting increasingly cozy with government.

281
00:42:26,160 --> 00:42:38,800
So how does one preserve democratic ideals in an environment, a medium environment that kind of tends naturally towards autocratic behavior.

282
00:42:43,600 --> 00:43:00,160
And one thing I would just say on this point is that the thing, one advantage that maybe the autocracies have over democracies here is that they are very concerned with controlling the technology and making sure that the technology serves someone, which is their own government.

283
00:43:01,760 --> 00:43:08,640
Whereas in free societies, we're much more open to having people design the technology however they want.

284
00:43:08,880 --> 00:43:10,720
They can design it to benefit their own company.

285
00:43:10,960 --> 00:43:22,160
They can design it to say only nice things about the founder of the company and all those sorts of things, but at least there are kind of multiple perspectives there.

286
00:43:24,480 --> 00:43:42,400
This gives a focus to autocracies that democracies don't have, but I think democracies could do a little bit to defend themselves here by saying, you know, let's make sure that these technologies are something that we are aware of in terms of their power and in terms of what they can do to manipulate public opinion.

287
00:43:45,040 --> 00:43:49,920
We need to be aware that outsiders can come into our information system and try to influence us.

288
00:43:50,960 --> 00:43:53,440
And that becomes training data then for the AIs.

289
00:43:53,920 --> 00:44:05,200
What do we have to do in order to protect our own artificial intelligences from taking up a bunch of external propaganda and then spouting propaganda as something that is a genuine possibility?

290
00:44:06,160 --> 00:44:13,520
Now, hopefully, most of the post training that happens to AIs will get rid of a lot of that, but it's not certainly not going to be all of it.

291
00:44:14,720 --> 00:44:23,280
And so having an open society and trying to protect an open society is something that democracies have been thinking about for a long time, ever since we've been competing with autocracies.

292
00:44:24,880 --> 00:44:32,640
And these sorts of questions are not easy to solve because we are, you know, one of the other fundamental things about an open society is that we tend to trust each other, right?

293
00:44:33,040 --> 00:44:38,000
If you grow up in the United States, you're more likely to trust people than maybe if you grow up in an autocracy.

294
00:44:38,400 --> 00:44:45,120
And then if an autocracy comes in from the outside and starts influencing the way things happen in your country, you're much too open to their influence.

295
00:44:45,360 --> 00:44:49,600
You really should be more skeptical of things that you're being exposed to.

296
00:44:51,440 --> 00:44:51,520
Yeah.

297
00:44:51,680 --> 00:45:15,120
I mean, I think if we're talking about really protecting democracy that, you know, I've heard people advocate for something like a second amendment for compute, like how do we create an open source opportunity for people to engage in this kind of development and work instead of, you know, sort of, you know, arguing online about what Dario Amade is doing at Anthropic as if that's sort of the end all be all.

298
00:45:15,360 --> 00:45:29,200
You know, this is a company that, and Brian, maybe you could speak to this because the Constitution is referred to a few times, but at one point in the essay, he says, people don't understand the intricacies of developing AI day to day.

299
00:45:29,520 --> 00:45:30,880
They can't possibly understand it.

300
00:45:31,120 --> 00:45:36,320
And it would be naive to say that we can apply first principles to this kind of work.

301
00:45:37,040 --> 00:45:37,920
It's too complex.

302
00:45:38,560 --> 00:45:43,520
And it would also be naive to create a clean sounding story about how all this works.

303
00:45:43,840 --> 00:45:53,360
And then ten pages later, we hear about a constitution that is plain spoken so we can all read it and operates on first principles.

304
00:45:53,680 --> 00:45:56,560
And so he just keeps contradicting himself through the essay.

305
00:45:56,720 --> 00:46:00,400
And I just wonder, as someone who worked on the constitution, what your feelings about that are.

