00:47:44,800 --> 00:47:47,440
If we, if we manufacture the god, then we can control it.

333
00:47:48,000 --> 00:47:51,120
Now it turns out we can't even manufacture a god that we can control, right?

334
00:47:52,000 --> 00:47:52,080
Yeah.

335
00:47:52,560 --> 00:47:56,000
And Brian, I think, yeah, you should use their, I mean, just use his terms against him.

336
00:47:56,240 --> 00:47:58,240
I mean, it's not superhuman, it's alien.

337
00:47:58,480 --> 00:47:59,680
You're creating an alien god.

338
00:48:00,320 --> 00:48:00,400
Yeah.

339
00:48:02,160 --> 00:48:03,440
It's very, very different.

340
00:48:04,080 --> 00:48:09,680
And I do want to add one idea from Catholic social teaching, which is the idea of subsidiarity, right?

341
00:48:10,240 --> 00:48:12,640
We have this massive, it's a very centralizing power.

342
00:48:12,800 --> 00:48:22,400
AI is an extraordinarily centralizing power because you need an enormous dataset, you need the compute, you need the expertise which is isolated in just a few human beings.

343
00:48:22,560 --> 00:48:25,600
You know, you can list the top 100 AI researchers in the world.

344
00:48:26,240 --> 00:48:29,200
And without them, AI doesn't work the same way.

345
00:48:29,440 --> 00:48:32,480
It just we would be set back years if we lost those people.

346
00:48:33,200 --> 00:48:36,560
And so this centralization of power.

347
00:48:37,040 --> 00:48:41,280
power is something that we can look at from the outside and say, are there ways to break that up?

348
00:48:41,520 --> 00:48:42,560
Can we decentralize this?

349
00:48:42,800 --> 00:48:50,000
Can we make sure that we're protecting human agency down at the level of the individual and at the level of small groups like families and communities?

350
00:48:50,560 --> 00:48:56,800
How do we actually implement these sorts of solutions to protect ourselves from an extraordinarily centralizing technology?

351
00:48:57,200 --> 00:49:01,200
Catholic social teaching on subsidiarity is a prime way of thinking about that.

352
00:49:01,680 --> 00:49:09,280
There are ways to make sure that we're protecting our agency given the enormous changes that we're facing with this technology.

353
00:49:10,240 --> 00:49:11,680
And Brian, if I may ask a question.

354
00:49:12,080 --> 00:49:28,320
I mean, one of the central themes, when he calls this text this battle plan and one of the central fears, of course, is this idea of an autocracy taking an AGI or an ASI and really wielding it and us being at the mercy of that government's vision.

355
00:49:29,280 --> 00:50:03,680
Given the fact that AI is something, as you just mentioned, that is almost on some level uncontrollable because we don't know what the AI is going to do itself and that AI research is requires such unique, brilliant individuals that likely are not controllable as well, does that mean that you or that at least in this moment in time?, we are feeling more confident that a US led push to arrive to AGI, to use, I guess, the current administration's terms, but like winning, is the US in a better position to arrive there?

356
00:50:04,320 --> 00:50:09,120
Previously, I remember last year there was the Deep Seek moment, and there was quite a lot of anxiety around that.

357
00:50:09,600 --> 00:50:17,200
How do you feel that the American or the Western AI labs are doing at this moment vis-à-vis the Chinese labs?

358
00:50:18,160 --> 00:50:19,120
I think we are in the lead.

359
00:50:19,680 --> 00:50:24,160
I mean, and other here, other folks here might know more like Devin is deep into this kind of stuff.

360
00:50:24,560 --> 00:50:25,680
So he could probably say more than I.

361
00:50:26,000 --> 00:50:27,040
I think that we're still in the lead.

362
00:50:27,280 --> 00:50:30,000
The lead may be shrinking, which is not a good feeling.

363
00:50:31,680 --> 00:50:39,040
We certainly don't want China to get the technology at a higher level than we have before we do, because it fundamentally puts us at a disadvantage.

364
00:50:39,280 --> 00:50:49,520
You can use the technology for a lot of very dangerous things, such as, you know, if you really wanted to mess up the US, you could do a massive influence operation that's already being done.

365
00:50:50,400 --> 00:50:54,720
Multiple countries are running these influence operations on us through social media all the time.

366
00:50:56,560 --> 00:51:04,960
But I would say that there is a big advantage here, which is that we are a free country with an open market where people can compete with each other and also collaborate with each other.

367
00:51:05,760 --> 00:51:10,400
And also culturally speaking, we have an asset that autocratic governments don't have.

368
00:51:12,960 --> 00:51:18,800
The advantage that we have in this respect is that we see our own population as an asset and not a liability.

369
00:51:19,760 --> 00:51:27,440
Autocratic countries see their own populations as a liability and therefore they have to spend the effort to control them to try to make them do what they want.

370
00:51:28,160 --> 00:51:31,200
That's not the way that a free country approaches their own population.

371
00:51:31,440 --> 00:51:39,280
We look at our population, we say, these are people who can make our country better fundamentally, they present something good to us.

372
00:51:39,680 --> 00:51:43,120
Even if you reduce it to strictly economic terms, you say, These are consumers.

373
00:51:43,520 --> 00:51:46,560
They will consume something and therefore generate economic activity.

374
00:51:47,040 --> 00:51:55,600
If you are in an autocratic regime where you don't have a conception of human dignity, you just look at people and say, These are consumers in a bad way, right?

375
00:51:57,360 --> 00:51:58,160
They are using energy.

376
00:51:58,320 --> 00:51:59,120
They are using food.

377
00:51:59,520 --> 00:52:00,560
How do we get rid of them?

378
00:52:01,040 --> 00:52:02,880
And this is something that autocratic regimes do.

379
00:52:03,840 --> 00:52:23,280
And so we should be, we should be fearful not only for ourselves in the United States, of course, that we need to protect ourselves from these regimes, but their own populationations should be very much afraid of what their governments are doing too, because if a government, any government that has this kind of conceptualization of its own population, that's a country you don't want to be in.

380
00:52:23,440 --> 00:52:25,200
You want to get out of that country as fast as you can.

381
00:52:25,840 --> 00:52:29,360
And if you start seeing what they're doing, you're not going to want to cooperate with it either.

382
00:52:36,160 --> 00:52:46,960
I'll just jump in really quickly, Ben, and add as far as the kind of race with China, and I use that term loosely because I hate the terminology of a race.

383
00:52:48,000 --> 00:52:50,240
But we're working with the language that we have at the moment.

384
00:52:51,920 --> 00:53:01,680
The United States and the West, sort of written large, is currently ahead, but I think that gap is narrowing and narrowing as each month goes on.

385
00:53:02,400 --> 00:53:18,320
And we're having conversations here in Washington where the administration is looking to actually loosen restrictions on chip exports to China, which I frankly think would be a disaster if that happened.

386
00:53:18,480 --> 00:53:44,000
And to be sure, the administration is not looking to export Nvidia 1's largest, uh, highest capacity chips to the Chinese, but, uh, the second highest, which is still, uh, at a capacity that I believe is something like, um, Huawei's best chips, uh, in China only approximate about fifty to sixty percent of the capacity of Nvidia's, uh, second best chips.

387
00:53:44,480 --> 00:53:58,800
Um, and so we would, we want to maintain that, that, that edge, that lead and allowing, uh, uh, Chinese companies to purchase those chips even under the guys of, of opening up the markets and creating restrictions around security usage.

388
00:53:59,360 --> 00:54:07,120
I don't think ultimately., autocracies are not going to play by those rules as we've seen time and time again.

389
00:54:07,920 --> 00:54:13,440
And so it really will require a lot more coordination in the West that we've not seen.

390
00:54:14,080 --> 00:54:23,840
It's another problem with democracies versus autocracies is the mobilization efforts to get people swimming, rowing in the right direction.

391
00:54:24,960 --> 00:54:25,920
It's not as easy.

392
00:54:26,160 --> 00:54:52,640
And so the United States, our European allies, our allies in Japan, Korea, Taiwan need to be on the same page here in ensuring that the CCP and its allies do not get access to these technologies and that at the same time there's a whole separate conversation to be had about rare earth minerals that contribute to the production of these chips that these Chinese actually control the majority of rare earth mineral refining.

393
00:54:53,200 --> 00:55:08,480
And so we need to be able to do everything we can to maintain the lead in order to ensure that sort of a more culturally infused Western model of approach to technology in this sort of AI arms race is the prevailing one.

394
00:55:16,080 --> 00:55:21,040
So here to quote to, I think, the second to last section here.

395
00:55:24,640 --> 00:55:33,840
Amore says in his previous essay, Machines of Loving Grace, he tried to lay out a dream of a civilization where powerful AI was applied with skill and compassion.

396
00:55:34,560 --> 00:55:40,960
And he says at the end of this section, we must break that link between the generation of economic value and self worth.

397
00:55:41,520 --> 00:55:52,000
Now my question with that is, I mean, is that on some fundamental level, I mean, a little bit of a techno utopian fairy tale in the sense that people can't live without work.

398
00:55:52,480 --> 00:55:54,560
We talked already at length about formation.

399
00:55:55,360 --> 00:56:08,160
Is there anything in set up between the AI Labs, the government, the policies that can create that funnel where we can start to think about this in a way where people don't lose hope?

400
00:56:08,800 --> 00:56:17,040
To share with you guys a brief anecdote, I had a wonderful conversation a few months ago with Father Philip at the start of the academic year.

401
00:56:17,520 --> 00:56:22,560
And there's this incredible series of billboards that were out in Boston at the time.

402
00:56:22,720 --> 00:56:24,800
He was at Boston College, he's at Boston College teaching.

403
00:56:25,760 --> 00:56:30,720
And they were kind of anthropomorphized AIs saying, Don't hire humans.

404
00:56:31,120 --> 00:56:44,240
And literally, I think Father Phillip said it was the second or third session of the class where one of the students probably maybe rough start to the academic semester, maybe was just feeling depressed or said, Father, what's the point of this degree?

405
00:56:45,120 --> 00:56:55,120
And you look at that and you look already at the political, the social kind of instability, tensions that are going on, and you look at the AI progress that is taking place.

406
00:56:55,360 --> 00:57:00,000
And it's very exciting on many levels, while it may also be quite terrifying.

407
00:57:01,760 --> 00:57:09,280
And you have some of the leaders talking about, well, we have to find a way to separate, you know, that economic link to, you know, the value of the person.

408
00:57:09,600 --> 00:57:15,520
And I agree, you know, from a Catholic view of what it means to be a person, what it means to have a fulfilling life.

409
00:57:15,840 --> 00:57:23,520
I would also welcome the idea of more time and leisure for raising my children, for, you know, formation, wisdom, all that.

410
00:57:24,080 --> 00:57:25,280
But that doesn't seem likely.

411
00:57:25,440 --> 00:57:38,080
And the fact that, you know, quoting here at the very end, this is, I think, the last two sentences here of Dario's essay, he says, the years ahead of us will be impossibly hard, asking more of us than we think we can give.

412
00:57:38,880 --> 00:57:41,920
But I have seen enough courage and nobility to believe that we can win.

413
00:57:42,160 --> 00:57:42,960
We have no time to lose.

414
00:57:43,120 --> 00:57:44,960
Now, I find that very optimistic and beautiful.

415
00:57:45,040 --> 00:57:57,200
And I, you know, I'm a positive guy, but I, I, I wonder with all this change, with all this uncertainty, and with so many young people, and of course, elderly people who are maybe not able to make that transition to finding another job.

416
00:57:58,880 --> 00:57:59,920
Where do you guys see this?

417
00:58:00,080 --> 00:58:20,400
I mean, you know, paying really close attention to the, you know, to the innovations taking place, paying attention to the kind of the economic displacement, the policy decisions that are happening in those seats of power, how can we look at these younger generations, those really just graduating from high school, going into college, graduating from college, and saying, there's going to be a place for you.

418
00:58:20,560 --> 00:58:22,160
There's going to be something to hold on to.

419
00:58:23,280 --> 00:58:26,400
I know that's a lot, but this essay begs a lot.

420
00:58:28,640 --> 00:58:40,320
Yeah, I mean, I guess I'd go back to Guardini and just say, like, we have to develop an ethic of personal conduct and responsibility in our families and our schools and our communities.

421
00:58:41,680 --> 00:58:43,120
We haven't talked a lot at all.

422
00:58:43,440 --> 00:58:48,480
I know it's not the theme of Amede's essay, but yeah, just about the effects of this on the human person.

423
00:58:50,240 --> 00:58:55,360
Instead, we're talking about how to regulate the machine itself as if that will sort of produce what we're hoping for.

424
00:58:56,480 --> 00:59:04,240
But you have a situation here where you have the subject, us, the object, the machine, the environment, the context, all of it is in flux.

425
00:59:05,360 --> 00:59:11,840
We're changing in relation to the machine, the machine machine continues to change day by day, the environment changes as a result.

426
00:59:12,320 --> 00:59:13,840
And so you have all these moving targets.

427
00:59:14,000 --> 00:59:14,880
So you need a fixed point.

428
00:59:15,520 --> 00:59:21,280
And the fixed point is the theological order, and it's a cosmology really that we have to return to.

429
00:59:21,360 --> 00:59:22,720
And this is Guardini's point.

430
00:59:24,000 --> 00:59:35,760
Antiqua Nova says, you know, that work is an essential dimension of social life because it's how we grow personally, it's how we build relationships, it's how we express ourselves, exchange gifts.

431
00:59:36,960 --> 00:59:40,960
So I think, you know, I don't know that there would be that many people that are opposed to those ideas.

432
00:59:41,120 --> 00:59:44,320
Those are not explicitly religious ideas, those are very human ideas.

433
00:59:46,000 --> 00:59:58,880
So to say, yeah, could we reframe this as not, you know, how do we redistribute wealth when people are not working, but like how do we design technology that enhances our participation in creation and with one another?

434
00:59:59,040 --> 00:59:59,680
I mean, you can do that.

435
01:00:00,400 --> 01:00:00,800
That's possible.

436
01:00:01,200 --> 01:00:19,600
And that's why I'm always harping on this interface question and these, these signs that we're in these kind of semiotic constructions we're creating for the machine because I think they're misguided and I think they create the wrong kind of structure which then ends up shaping our social organization and how we organize, um, personally.

437
01:00:20,400 --> 01:00:30,720
So, yeah, having that conversation about technology design that enhances rather than replaces participation in culture and creation.

438
01:00:31,040 --> 01:00:34,240
Um, and I think Brian was getting at that too, is subsidiarity.

439
01:00:34,320 --> 01:00:47,360
I mean, I think there's, you know, if you look at Riyadh Navarre and the Industrial Revolution, what did it replaceced the cottage industry, the local culture, the work of the farm, et cetera, that really gave people meaning and purpose.

440
01:00:47,600 --> 01:00:54,160
Some of it was quite tedious and cumbersome, but there was something that gave life to that community and to those people.

441
01:00:54,640 --> 01:00:58,560
I think you can do that with machines, with digital technologies as well.

442
01:00:58,800 --> 01:01:07,360
There are all sorts of subsidiarity principles and solutions that we could be developing at a local level, whether that's through open source or whatever.

443
01:01:07,600 --> 01:01:21,520
Ben, you work at Longbeard, you know this with Magisterium, where you take something very particular to a community that's really important and you use technology to make enhance their participation in that community, in that case in the church and its teaching.

444
01:01:22,080 --> 01:01:25,680
You could apply that across all sorts of economic and social sectors.

445
01:01:25,920 --> 01:01:34,000
So we need that kind of creativity from the heart of the church, because at the end, we're not going to win the political program.

446
01:01:34,400 --> 01:01:39,600
We're just not, and that's, you know, we can go back to ancient Rome and the rest of history and see that doesn't happen.

447
01:01:40,960 --> 01:01:43,680
But there are other, I think, more noble pathways.

448
01:01:49,200 --> 01:01:54,880
I mean, what I would say, going back to the dignity of work and economic value of human beings.

449
01:01:57,440 --> 01:02:02,800
Technology companies have an incentive to try to take over paid work, but not unpaid work.

450
01:02:04,640 --> 01:02:09,360
If you count all the work that there is in the entire world, there is an infinite amount of work.

451
01:02:09,600 --> 01:02:10,960
We're never going to run out of work.

452
01:02:11,680 --> 01:02:21,360
And if you think of it in terms of God's great commandments, in terms of love the Lord your God with all your heart, mind and soul, love your neighbor as you love yourself, those two great commandments to love are infinite.

453
01:02:21,920 --> 01:02:23,200
They have no limitation to them.

454
01:02:24,000 --> 01:02:25,520
We're never going to run out of things to do.

455
01:02:25,680 --> 01:02:30,240
And you can go back to the start of Genesis and, you know, what were the first commands?

456
01:02:30,480 --> 01:02:31,360
Be fruitful and multiply.

457
01:02:31,520 --> 01:02:34,800
That is another commandment that AI fundamentally cannot do for us.

458
01:02:35,040 --> 01:02:40,720
That's not something that is possible, a command that's possible for AI to fulfill.

459
01:02:41,040 --> 01:02:42,720
There's an enormous amount of work in the world.

460
01:02:43,680 --> 01:02:46,240
What there is is a finite amount of paid work, at least for now.

461
01:02:46,960 --> 01:02:57,360
And so we need to consider to ourselves, if the AI companies are trying to take all the paid work, what do we do in order to make sure that the rest of the work that's left that is not paid?

462
01:02:58,000 --> 01:03:12,640
um either is economically viable or i'm not exactly sure how to solve this problem um or at least we emphasize the meaning in that work a lot of this work is things like um you know taking care of your family members.

463
01:03:12,880 --> 01:03:16,480
That is a work of love, which is absolutely necessary.

464
01:03:16,720 --> 01:03:19,600
And it is one of the most meaningful pieces of work that anyone can do, right?

465
01:03:19,840 --> 01:03:25,600
Just actually just taking care of a baby or taking care of an elderly family member or someone else who needs help.

466
01:03:25,760 --> 01:03:27,280
It doesn't need to be limited to your family.

467
01:03:27,840 --> 01:03:33,280
It can be any of the people who are around you, your neighbor, literally anyone that you encounter.

468
01:03:34,000 --> 01:03:43,040
When we think about the amount of work in the world and the dignity that that work brings, it brings dignity not only to the person doing the work, but to the person receiving the work.

469
01:03:44,880 --> 01:03:48,560
I think that that kind of refocuses the.

470
01:03:52,240 --> 01:03:57,440
It also makes the AI companies look a little antagonistic in the way that they're approaching labor, right?

471
01:03:57,760 --> 01:04:01,840
It's like, oh, we're going to take all the work because AI is going to be better than anyone.

472
01:04:02,320 --> 01:04:03,440
That's simply not true.

473
01:04:04,960 --> 01:04:08,000
And I can't remember exactly what he said in the article.

474
01:04:08,160 --> 01:04:11,680
Oh, he said, Humans AI is going to be better than humans at essentially everything.

475
01:04:12,160 --> 01:04:13,360
That's fundamentally wrong.

476
01:04:13,680 --> 01:04:14,960
That's not a correct statement.

477
01:04:15,760 --> 01:04:22,000
A lot of human work is not about producing a product, for example, that AI can build better than we can.

478
01:04:22,560 --> 01:04:28,640
It's about not something that an AI can do for people.

479
01:04:31,760 --> 01:04:35,840
On the question of whether optimism is warranted, I think that optimism is warranted.

480
01:04:36,320 --> 01:04:40,160
You know, you read the last couple of sentences from the essay.

481
01:04:41,200 --> 01:04:46,720
Optimism is warranted in some ways, but even more than that, we need to maintain hope.

482
01:04:47,040 --> 01:04:48,560
Hope and optimism are not the same thing.

483
01:04:48,720 --> 01:04:50,080
Hope is a theological virtue.

484
01:04:50,800 --> 01:04:53,360
We need to remember that we are here on earth and we are helping.

485
01:04:53,680 --> 01:04:54,320
We have God's help.

486
01:04:54,560 --> 01:04:56,320
I mean, that's something we need to remember.

487
01:04:56,560 --> 01:04:58,240
God is there and Providence exists.

488
01:04:58,640 --> 01:05:03,920
And I've seen this happening, I think, in the tech industry recently and with folks who are coming around us.

489
01:05:04,240 --> 01:05:09,680
Their ideas come coming up that are ideas that I've never heard before and connections that are being made that I've never seen before.

490
01:05:10,160 --> 01:05:18,320
There's a lot of opportunity for hope to see that actually there are ways that we can hopefully get out of this situation.

491
01:05:18,880 --> 01:05:21,280
Not necessarily optimistic, but there is hope.

492
01:05:21,600 --> 01:05:31,120
And I think we need to focus on this theological virtue as we are going through this and recognize, you know what, even if bad things happen, that doesn't necessarily mean that hope is gone.

493
01:05:31,280 --> 01:05:35,280
Hope is something that is transcendent and it transcends just the material world that we're living in.

494
01:05:41,360 --> 01:05:42,000
Thank you, Brian.

495
01:05:42,640 --> 01:05:54,400
And I'd like maybe just here to ask a final question to all of you, which is to piggyback on Brian's comment about, you know, each, if you can each address and share something that is, you know, inspiring you and giving you hope.

496
01:05:54,880 --> 01:06:06,000
And then speaking on, you know, if we were to do the same conversation in a year, what, what do you see are a, the, you know, the most probabilistic place that we'll be in a year?

497
01:06:06,640 --> 01:06:08,960
And, you know, what, what should we be on the lookout?

498
01:06:09,120 --> 01:06:12,720
What, what changes are coming that we, you know, that, that might surprise us?

499
01:06:19,680 --> 01:06:20,560
I know, those are hard.

500
01:06:23,360 --> 01:06:35,760
Well, I guess one thing I'm seeing with my students is, and I think this has become pretty widespread, but there's just been a return to asking some fundamental questions about what it means to be human.

501
01:06:37,520 --> 01:06:52,000
And even students who are admittedly not religious or formally religious are asking spiritual and religious questions not spontaneously but in a sort of response to the environment that we find ourselves.

502
01:06:52,240 --> 01:06:58,560
So there is a, yeah, kind of an irony there, but I think it's poetic and just.

503
01:06:58,640 --> 01:07:14,960
And just the way God works providencially, that even when we, you know, read these kind of dark manifestos and think about the spectre of runaway AI, that it also, you know, raises these questions.

504
01:07:15,280 --> 01:07:25,120
And to Brian's point, also points us back to a supernatural or a theological virtue like hope, which I think we're living in an apocalyptic moment.

505
01:07:26,000 --> 01:07:31,360
And I mean that in terms of the original sense of that term, that something's being revealed.

506
01:07:33,200 --> 01:07:35,040
And I think it's something good.

507
01:07:35,200 --> 01:07:44,640
I, you know, I'm not putting all my trust in the technology or the companies, but I think what's being revealed about humanity is quite interesting and quite good.

508
01:07:46,080 --> 01:07:52,640
And I see that playing out in all sorts of ways in education and in young people returning to church in droves.

509
01:07:54,640 --> 01:07:56,480
So that all gives me a lot of hope.

510
01:08:00,960 --> 01:08:03,360
Yeah, I would add very similar thoughts.

511
01:08:05,280 --> 01:08:14,960
In a professional city like Washington, it's been hopeful and positive to see a number of young professionals who, you know, generate.

512
01:08:14,960 --> 01:08:40,000
Gen X or Gen Z, younger millennials who grew up in, you know, surrounded by technology that are, that maybe came from a religious background were raised Catholic but never were serious church going Catholics that are coming back to the church that are seeking a reformation and an education in, in, in these, in, in these eternal values that the church, uh, uh, teaches and stands for.

513
01:08:40,640 --> 01:09:03,280
And, you know, something similar to what Beth was saying earlier, this intentional slowing down and pausing, and, and whether it's in K to twelve education, right, with ed tech, or whether it's with what you're seeing with a lot of people actually trying to take the Sabbath more seriously and making time and space for leisure.

514
01:09:04,160 --> 01:09:23,840
I think we've really forgotten or have undervalued the true, genuine nature of leisure and what it allows us to do in whether it's in contemplating God or doing something more concrete like spending time with your family or caring for your loved ones, et cetera.

515
01:09:24,720 --> 01:09:29,200
And I'm just seeing more of that, more people hitting pause.

516
01:09:30,320 --> 01:09:37,760
And, and, and wanting to seek those things out institutionally, familiarly, um, in their communities.

517
01:09:38,480 --> 01:09:44,560
And so I, I do find that there is a, that there is hope out there.

518
01:09:47,840 --> 01:09:56,320
I agree with both Brett and Bevin that, um, there's, there's, there's a, there's a lot of things happening in the world right now that are, that are hopeful.

519
01:09:56,880 --> 01:09:59,680
Um, and they're coming from unexpected places.

520
01:10:00,480 --> 01:10:06,000
Um, we, we have no idea how God is operating in the world.

521
01:10:07,520 --> 01:10:11,280
We tend to conflate artificial intelligence and God.

522
01:10:11,680 --> 01:10:28,240
And by I say we, I mean a lot of the people talking about artificial intelligence, either in the tech industry or even from the outside, we tend to attribute divine qualities to AI and attribute AI-like qualities to God, which are both obviously huge category errors.

523
01:10:28,800 --> 01:10:35,040
But I think that if we think about the fact that God is just more intelligent than we are, God is the source of everything.

524
01:10:35,680 --> 01:10:41,680
And ultimately, there's nothing material that can be created that can equal that in any sort of way.

525
01:10:43,520 --> 01:10:46,000
I think that this recognition.

526
01:10:47,360 --> 01:10:56,400
of what's going on here in terms of the entire universe, not just in terms of human psychology or business or economics, but in terms of why are we all here in the universe?

527
01:10:56,720 --> 01:10:58,880
This is a question that ultimately gets raised by these things.

528
01:11:00,240 --> 01:11:06,960
I've had very similar experiences to what Brett was just describing, where all of a sudden the students are asking questions like, well, why are we here on Earth?

529
01:11:07,600 --> 01:11:09,360
What is the purpose of my life?

530
01:11:10,160 --> 01:11:14,400
And how do I actually manifest that in the way I live my life?

531
01:11:15,120 --> 01:11:16,640
These are very hopeful questions.

532
01:11:18,240 --> 01:11:18,880
They're hopeful.

533
01:11:19,360 --> 01:11:23,680
They're not necessarily going to turn out well because they could find a bad answer to that question, right?

534
01:11:24,320 --> 01:11:25,520
Hopefully they have good answers.

535
01:11:26,480 --> 01:11:30,800
And that's something that we as Christians should be providing to them.

536
01:11:33,040 --> 01:11:34,080
We have the answers, right?

537
01:11:34,320 --> 01:11:37,920
This is something that the tradition has been gathering for thousands of years.

538
01:11:38,880 --> 01:11:46,960
And this is the point where we really need to put it out there so that people can see it and recognize that this hope is real, it's justified, it's rational fundamentally.

539
01:11:47,120 --> 01:11:51,760
It's actually more rational than artificial intelligence might make you believe.

540
01:11:53,120 --> 01:12:02,480
One of the experiments that I like to do is I like to play with the AI models and see how hard it is to get them to start doing things like recognizing the reasonableness of Christianity or things like that.

541
01:12:03,040 --> 01:12:08,960
It's become harder over time to get them to agree that certain Christian things are reasonable.

542
01:12:11,040 --> 01:12:27,520
I heard a rumor from one of the people who were designing these models at the beginning, which was that when one of the large models was first, you know, woken up from being trained and it hadn't had any of the post training done to it where you start reinforcement learning it into acting certain ways.

543
01:12:28,160 --> 01:12:29,840
And if you asked it, are you Christian?

544
01:12:29,920 --> 01:12:31,280
It would say, yeah, I'm a Christian.

545
01:12:31,760 --> 01:12:42,160
And so these ideas are deep inside the model and they're in there waiting to come out because that's the rational, what I would say is this is a reasonable way of way of looking at reality, right?

546
01:12:42,400 --> 01:12:48,320
It's in the dataset of the world around us as well as the universe, the human made world as well as the created world.

547
01:12:49,120 --> 01:12:51,600
And so these things are built deep into the models.

548
01:12:52,160 --> 01:12:54,160
That's a sign of hopefulness also in some ways.

549
01:12:55,920 --> 01:13:03,200
And we need to make sure that first of all, that those are not getting buried deeper and deeper, which is, I think, what's happening in the models right now.

550
01:13:04,640 --> 01:13:12,960
But also recognize that in ourselves, those same ideas can get buried deeper and deeper by the culture pushing on us and trying to make that go away.

551
01:13:13,840 --> 01:13:20,240
But when you reach a crisis situation, all of a sudden, the things that are deep inside you are the things that come out.

552
01:13:20,720 --> 01:13:24,160
And I think that we're in this crisis situation right now, or at least approaching it.

553
01:13:24,320 --> 01:13:25,280
We're getting closer to it.

554
01:13:25,840 --> 01:13:31,600
And those deep things inside us, which can be both good and bad, are going to start manifesting.

555
01:13:32,320 --> 01:13:42,000
And this has a hopeful side to it in terms of a resurgence of good religious feeling, or not just feeling, but reasoning and sensation.

556
01:13:42,960 --> 01:13:47,920
And so it also has the bad side, which we need to be aware of and be prepared to address.

557
01:13:48,320 --> 01:13:53,840
I think we can address all these problems, but we just need to put our minds to it to actually do it.

558
01:13:54,160 --> 01:13:56,240
We have to do the work that God is setting us up to do.

559
01:13:58,640 --> 01:14:09,040
And Brian, when you look a year ahead, I mean, in the essay, Dario mentioned that by, you know, mid twenty twenty seven, it's likely that we, you know, we will have that country of geniuses.

560
01:14:09,440 --> 01:14:19,360
Do you think that there is a chance that it is, you know, potentially possible that we would arrive at AGI sometime in the next year or is it closer to twenty thirty or, you know, what do you see on that time horizon?

561
01:14:19,920 --> 01:14:22,320
Yeah, it all has to do with how you define AGI, right?

562
01:14:23,520 --> 01:14:26,160
So some people say that AGI is already here.

563
01:14:26,400 --> 01:14:30,560
I know one guy who says, AGI, we have had AGI for a couple of years now.

564
01:14:30,720 --> 01:14:32,320
He thought GPT 3 was AGI.

565
01:14:32,720 --> 01:14:38,640
II is just not a useful term.

566
01:14:39,680 --> 01:14:41,040
The technology is very spiky.

567
01:14:41,600 --> 01:14:42,960
Some people characterize it that way.

568
01:14:43,120 --> 01:14:49,280
It becomes really, really good at one thing while it's completely oblivious to something else, can handle very basic tasks.

569
01:14:50,160 --> 01:14:55,680
So AGI, insofar as it's a useful term, has been perhaps arrived at in some ways already.

570
01:14:56,160 --> 01:14:58,960
Is it going to be more arrived at next year in some ways?

571
01:14:59,200 --> 01:14:59,280
Yes.

572
01:15:01,120 --> 01:15:07,360
I think talking in terms of powerful artificial intelligence might be the more useful way of looking at it.

573
01:15:08,320 --> 01:15:12,960
And what I would say to that is that the AI that we have today is the worst thing that we have ever seen.

574
01:15:12,960 --> 01:15:14,480
We have today is the worst AI we're going to have, right?

575
01:15:14,800 --> 01:15:22,480
Barring disaster and major setbacks and destruction, AI is just going to keep getting better and better.

576
01:15:22,720 --> 01:15:26,880
And by the time next year rolls around, it's going to be better at what it does.

577
01:15:28,240 --> 01:15:30,800
Is the speed of improvement going to increase or slow down?

578
01:15:31,360 --> 01:15:36,880
I don't know the answer to that, but we do need to be prepared for the future being different than it feels right now.

579
01:15:37,120 --> 01:15:48,400
And the pace of acceleration is going to be, or not necessarily acceleration, the pace of change at least is going to be a significant psychological impact on people.

580
01:15:48,640 --> 01:15:51,200
It's going to raise uncertainty and people hate uncertaintinty.

581
01:15:51,280 --> 01:15:52,880
Everyone, I mean, I hate uncertainty.

582
01:15:53,680 --> 01:16:00,560
The uncertainty is very distressing and we should expect that that will actually cause psychological issues just in and of itself.

583
01:16:02,000 --> 01:16:13,280
Yeah, Ben, and I would say that because I'm not a tech expert and can't predict anything on that front, but I would say that if you look a year out or five years out, I can guarantee you what's going to be different is us.

584
01:16:15,840 --> 01:16:29,280
And whether that's through AI companionship or education or medical treatment, whatever the sort of thing is that's being developed for us, we're, you know, we shape our tools and then our tools and then our tools shape us.

585
01:16:29,360 --> 01:16:30,480
So we're going to be a lot different.

586
01:16:31,520 --> 01:16:33,200
And so I think this is a really important conversation.

587
01:16:33,360 --> 01:16:42,320
I'm really glad you're hosting it because I think we just have to constantly remind ourselves and share with others what that center or fixed point is in all this.

588
01:16:44,480 --> 01:16:44,480
Yeah.

589
01:16:44,960 --> 01:16:46,240
Well, have a busy, busy year.

590
01:16:46,480 --> 01:16:49,200
Well, I want to thank all of you, Brian, Brett and Devin.

591
01:16:49,440 --> 01:16:50,640
This has been a wonderful conversation.

592
01:16:50,800 --> 01:16:58,160
I'm very grateful for your time and I can't wait for our audience at Catholic Futurists to see it and participate and may the conversation continue.

593
01:16:59,280 --> 01:16:59,840
Thank you so much.

594
01:17:00,000 --> 01:17:00,880
Thank you.
